/* book exam traps: An n-input LUT is not an n-input gate; it stores 2ⁿ truth-table bits and behaves like a small memory. | An n-input LUT needs 2ⁿ configuration bits, not n bits; a 4-input LUT needs 16 bits. | In a PAL the OR plane is fixed, so each output gets a fixed number of product terms — count them per output, not as a shared pool. In a PLA the product terms are shared, and each output ORs a programmable subset of them. | SRAM FPGA configuration memory is volatile like user SRAM, but it holds the logic configuration, not program data. | Routing and switch-box delay can dominate, so equal LUT counts do not guarantee equal speed. */
